{"name":"Attitude","hex":"#a48884","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"attitude","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The position of the body or way of carrying oneself.","A disposition or state of mind."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To assume or to place in a particular position or orientation; to pose.","To express an attitude through one's posture, bearing, tone of voice, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/attitude"}],"entries":[{"title":"Attitude (psychology)","blocks":[{"text":"In psychology, an attitude \"is a summary evaluation of an object of thought. An attitude object can be anything a person discriminates or holds in mind\". Attitudes include beliefs (cognition), emotional responses (affect) and behavioral tendencies. In the classical definition an attitude is persistent, while in more contemporary conceptualizations, attitudes may vary depending upon situations, context, or moods."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Two_girls_talking.jpg/330px-Two_girls_talking.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attitude_(psychology)","wikidata":"Q622645"},{"title":"Attitude change","blocks":[{"text":"Attitude change is when a person or group changes their views, values, or beliefs about a particular topic, issue, or object. This can happen as a result of new information, experiences, or influence from others. Attitudes are associated beliefs and behaviors towards some object. They are not stable, and because of the communication and behavior of other people, are subject to change by social influences, as well as by the individual's motivation to maintain cognitive consistency when cognitive dissonance occurs—when two attitudes or attitude and behavior conflict. Attitudes and attitude objects are functions of affective and cognitive components. It has been suggested that the inter-structural composition of an associative network can be altered by the activation of a single node. Thus, by activating an affective or emotional node, attitude change may be possible, though affective and cognitive components tend to be intertwined."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attitude_change","wikidata":"Q4818546"},{"title":"Attitude (heraldry)","blocks":[{"text":"In heraldry, the term attitude describes the position in which a figure is emblazoned as a charge, a supporter, or as a crest. The attitude of a heraldic figure always precedes any reference to the tincture of the figure and its parts. Some attitudes apply only to predatory beasts, exemplified by the beast most usual to heraldry – the heraldic lion; other terms apply to docile animals, such as the doe, usually emblazoned as a \"hind\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Lion_Passant_Guardant.svg/330px-Lion_Passant_Guardant.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attitude_(heraldry)","wikidata":"Q1243409"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Asch_experiment.png/500px-Asch_experiment.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Asch_experiment.png/500px-Asch_experiment.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of the pairs of cards used in the experiment. The card on the left has the reference line and the one on the right shows the three comparison lines.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAsch_experiment.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Complete_Guide_to_Heraldry_Fig292.png/120px-Complete_Guide_to_Heraldry_Fig292.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Complete_Guide_to_Heraldry_Fig292.png/